module gunbc.compute.remote_build_hosts

import std.types { Bool, List }
import product.host_identity { HostIdentity }

// THE HOSTS THAT SERVE REMOTE BUILDS, AS THE ONE ROW BOTH SIDES OF THE SHARE READ. A serving host runs
// the remote-build account (gunbc.fleet.fleet_posix_accounts fleet_posix_remote_build_user) beside the
// operator's service user, and both reserve memory in the host's ONE capacity ledger. So two facts
// follow from membership here, and both are derived from this list rather than restated:
//   - gunbc.compute.host_capacity groups that host's ledger directories to the ledger group, so the
//     remote-build account can write them (compute_ledger_sharing); AND
//   - gunbc.compute.host_capacity host_capacity_home_for_host resolves that host's ledger to the
//     host-scoped home instead of walking a dashboard instance, and gunbc.compute.remote_build puts the
//     host's serving instance on the roster.
// It sits below both modules because host_capacity cannot import the roster (the roster reaches the
// provider, which reaches host_capacity).
//
// ADMISSION IS FAIL-CLOSED: A HOST IS ON THIS LIST ONLY WHEN ITS LEDGER HOME ALREADY EXISTS ON DISK,
// created and read back. gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed ("every path succeeds fully or fails with a
// typed, located diagnostic") and gunbc.design_argument::replacement_migration ("atomic describes the
// authority transition"): if a host were listed before its /opt/gunbc/compute/capacity existed, dispatch
// would resolve a ledger in a directory nothing made, and -- worse -- the host's OWN dashboard instances
// would start resolving their ledger to /opt/gunbc via host_capacity_home_for_host and the host's live
// compute provider would refuse, because the host home is not there yet. So the row and the directory
// are landed in ONE change: the converger that CREATES and READS BACK the home is the thing that adds
// the row. That converger is remote-build PR 5 (a separate child, gunbc.compute-serve-setup); it adopts
// srv1's existing home and creates the others.
//
// WHO IS LISTED, AND ON WHAT EVIDENCE. srv1 -- its home already existed (its dashboard instance lives at
// /opt/gunbc, so it does not migrate). srv3 and srv4 -- admitted 2026-11-18 once the serving converger
// (gunbc.compute.remote_build_serving_converge) had created their account, ledger home, store, checkout,
// rustup, toolchain and serve binary and re-observed each host settled (fleet-converge runs 44198068780 and
// 44203229183, confirmed for srv3 by the independent observe 44199766267). Listing them carves one CI runner
// slot on each into its build pool (gunbc.runner.runner_slot_desired host_build_pool_reservation): they
// carry no dashboard instance to hold their own reservation. srv2 is NOT listed: its runner is down and its
// serving setup is unobserved. Its ledger switch from /home/briansrls to the host home is the converger's
// actuation -- no in-flight reservation carries across the switch, so it should happen when srv2 has no
// builds in flight.
//
// FOLLOW-UP, NOT INVENTED HERE. butch (srv14) and bobo (srv15) have the operator's interest but not yet
// a place on this list, and the new Supermicro is being modeled in another lane (still-fox-404):
//   - bobo (srv15) has no anchored known-hosts row (the last real scan covered srv1-4 and butch), so it
//     cannot be dialed; adding one needs a key-scan observation with provenance, not a key from memory.
//   - butch (srv14) has an anchored row but is a GPU serving host with no CI runner width and no modeled
//     build ceiling, so a build-pool figure for it would be a guess (gunbc.design_argument::
//     epistemic_humility); it is held off until its role gives an honest number.
//   - the Supermicro joins when it has both an identity with an anchored row and a declared pool.
// Each joins by adding a row here the day it has an on-disk ledger home, an anchored row and a real pool
// figure -- never before.
fn remote_build_serving_hosts() -> List<HostIdentity> {
  [
    "srv1" as HostIdentity,
    "srv3" as HostIdentity,
    "srv4" as HostIdentity,
  ]
}

fn remote_build_host_is_serving(host: HostIdentity) -> Bool {
  remote_build_serving_hosts() |> any(h => h as String == host as String)
}
